1. Aim
To implement Brute-Force String Matching and investigate how text and pattern structure affects the number of character comparisons when searching cybersecurity logs for known event signatures.
2. Algorithm
Brute-Force String Matching checks the pattern at every possible position in the text. If a character does not match, it shifts the pattern one position to the right and starts again.

Prediction: The number of comparisons depends not only on the text length and pattern length but also on how many characters match before a mismatch occurs.
3. Three Designed Test Conditions
Test	Text	Pattern	Predicted behaviour
1. Easy match	XYZABC	ABC	Few comparisons because the pattern matches near the end
2. Repeated characters	AAAAAAAAAA	AAAAB	Many comparisons because four characters match before each mismatch
3. Immediate mismatch	BBBBBBBBBB	AAA	Few comparisons because the first character mismatches at every position

4. Python Implementation

def brute_force_search(text, pattern):
    comparisons = 0
    n = len(text)
    m = len(pattern)

    for i in range(n - m + 1):
        j = 0

        while j < m:
            comparisons += 1

            if text[i + j] != pattern[j]:
                break

            j += 1

        if j == m:
            return i, comparisons

    return -1, comparisons


