7. Analysis
Why does Test 2 require more work?
- The text contains repeated A characters.
- The pattern AAAAB matches four characters before failing on B.
- The algorithm repeats these four successful comparisons at each possible position.
- Therefore, it performs 30 comparisons, compared with only 8 in Test 3.
Why does Test 3 require less work?
- The text contains only B characters, while the pattern begins with A.
- The first character mismatches at every position.
- The algorithm stops checking the pattern immediately at each position.
What does this reveal about input size?

Two searches with similar text lengths can require very different numbers of comparisons. The arrangement of repeated characters and matching prefixes influences the computational effort, so input size alone cannot predict the exact work performed.
8. Time Complexity
Let \(n\) be the text length and \(m\) be the pattern length.
- Best case: \(O(m)\), when the pattern matches at the first position.
- Worst case: \(O((n-m+1)m)\), or \(O(nm)\), when many characters match before mismatches occur at repeated positions.
- Space complexity: \(O(1)\) auxiliary space.
9. Conclusion
Brute-Force String Matching is simple to implement, but its performance depends on both input size and character arrangement. Repeated prefixes can cause many unnecessary comparisons. For large cybersecurity logs, algorithms such as Knuth–Morris–Pratt (KMP) can reduce repeated checking and improve search efficiency.
